limits of the source
  The BSD package of this release is a FAT build: its architecture field reads "vFATAp2-A", the A-profile architecture plus Arm's
  future architecture technologies (FAT). A table generator selects the extensions masc supports and does not take the whole file.
  Descriptions are redacted in the BSD package, so the data holds encodings, assembly rules, operations and registers but not the
  text of the Architecture Reference Manual.
  Instructions.json carries A64, A32 and T32. masc reads A64 only.

how it is checked
  The source is a row of gen's source table with this revision and the checksum of the vendored tree, so gen --check refuses a copy
  that has changed. Nothing is fetched at generation time. A later release is a new copy and a new row in one commit.

the release the overlay cites
  The overlay under gen/data/aarch64 reads each instruction's operands, roles and effects from its page of Arm's A64 ISA XML,
  whose pseudocode the BSD package redacts. That release is not vendored, because its notice forbids redistribution. Its
  ISA_A64_xml_A_profile-2026-09/notice.xml reads:

    No part of this document may be reproduced in any form by any means without the express prior written permission of Arm
    Limited ("Arm"). No license, express or implied, by estoppel or otherwise to any intellectual property rights is granted by
    this document unless specifically stated.

  So it is a cited source, the row A64_ISA_XML of gen/src/registry.mach, which gen refuses unless it states every field below.
  Only names and facts are read from the pages, and no text of them is copied.

